1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,070
TUTOR: So, Rosie and Martin, let's look at what you've got for your presentation on woolly mammoths.

2
00:00:10,070 --> 00:00:14,900
ROSIE: OK, we've got a short outline here.

3
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:22,380
TUTOR: Thanks. So it's about a research project in North America?

4
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:28,927
MARTIN: Yes. But we thought we needed something general about woolly mammoths in our introduction,

5
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:33,292
to establish that they were related to our modern elephant,

6
00:00:33,292 --> 00:00:38,530
and they lived thousands of years ago in the last ice age.

7
00:00:38,530 --> 00:00:43,944
ROSIE: Maybe we could show a video clip of a cartoon about mammoths.

8
00:00:43,944 --> 00:00:46,443
But that'd be a bit childish.

9
00:00:46,443 --> 00:00:48,240
Or we could have a diagram

10
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:53,940
it could be a timeline to show when they lived, with illustrations?

11
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:57,480
MARTIN: Or we could just show a drawing of them walking in the ice

12
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,350
No, let's go with your last suggestion.

13
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:10,108
TUTOR: Good. Then you're describing the discovery of the mammoth tooth on St Paul's Island in Alaska,

14
00:01:10,108 --> 00:01:12,390
and why it was significant.

15
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:17,793
ROSIE: Yes. The tooth was found by a man called Russell Graham.

16
00:01:18,456 --> 00:01:22,272
He picked it up from under a rock in a cave

17
00:01:22,272 --> 00:01:28,287
He knew it was special - for a start it was in really good condition,

18
00:01:28,287 --> 00:01:30,660
as if it had been just extracted from the animals jawbone

19
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:36,880
Anyway, they found it was 6, 500 years old.

20
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:40,300
TUTOR: So why was that significant?

21
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:48,674
ROSIE: Well the mammoth bones previously found on the North American mainland were much less recent than that.

22
00:01:48,674 --> 00:01:51,000
So this was really amazing.

23
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,953
MARTIN: Then we're making an animated diagram to show the geography of the area in prehistoric times.

24
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:05,503
So originally, St Paul's Island wasn't an island, it was connected to the mainland,

25
00:02:05,503 --> 00:02:12,520
and mammoths and other animals like bears were able to roam around the whole area.

26
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:17,664
ROSIE: Then the climate warmed up and the sea level began to rise,

27
00:02:17,664 --> 00:02:21,225
and the island got cut off from the mainland.

28
00:02:21,225 --> 00:02:27,160
So those mammoths on the island couldn't escape: they had to stay on the island.

29
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:35,310
MARTIN: And in fact the species survived there for thousands of years after they'd become extinct on the mainland.

30
00:02:35,310 --> 00:02:40,310
TUTOR: So why do you think they died out on the mainland?

31
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:43,000
ROSIE: No one's sure.

32
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,036
MARTIN: Anyway, next we'll explain how Graham

33
00:02:46,036 --> 00:02:52,109
and his team identified the date when the mammoths became extinct on the island.

34
00:02:52,109 --> 00:02:56,446
They concluded that the extinction happened 5,600 years ago,

35
00:02:56,446 --> 00:03:00,784
which is a very precise time for a prehistoric extinction.

36
00:03:00,784 --> 00:03:08,158
It's based on samples they took from mud at the bottom of a lake on the island.

37
00:03:08,158 --> 00:03:13,364
They analysed it to find out what had fallen in over time -

38
00:03:13,364 --> 00:03:18,569
bits of plants, volcanic ash and even DNA from the mammoths themselves.

39
00:03:18,569 --> 00:03:23,340
It's standard procedure, but it took nearly two years to do.

40
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:29,487
TUTOR: So why don't you quickly go through the main sections of your presentation

41
00:03:29,487 --> 00:03:33,000
