51
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:20,724
MARTIN: Great idea. What about the section with the initial questions asked by the researchers?

52
00:04:20,724 --> 00:04:25,910
We've got a lot on that but we need to make it interesting.

53
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:29,998
ROSIE: We could ask the audience to suggest some questions about it

54
00:04:29,998 --> 00:04:36,130
and then see how many of them we can answer. I don't think it would take too long.

55
00:04:36,130 --> 00:04:39,790
TUTOR: Yes, that would add a bit of variety.

56
00:04:39,790 --> 00:04:44,940
MARTIN: Then the section on further research carried out on the island

57
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:47,440
analysing the mud in the lake.

58
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:53,390
I wonder if we've actually got too much information here, should we cut some?

59
00:04:53,390 --> 00:04:58,760
ROSIE: I don't think so, but it's all a bit muddled at present.

60
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:04,270
MARTIN: Yes, maybe it would be better if it followed a chronological pattern.

61
00:05:04,270 --> 00:05:07,080
ROSIE: I think so. The findings

62
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:10,021
and possible explanations section is just about ready,

63
00:05:10,021 --> 00:05:14,950
but we need to practise it so were sure it won't overrun.

64
00:05:14,950 --> 00:05:20,170
MARTIN: I think it should be OK, but yes, let's make sure.

65
